@page "/your-plan"
<PageTitle>Your plan · Tangent Atlas</PageTitle>

<div class="ta-container ta-page">
    <h1 class="ta-visually-hidden">Your plan</h1>   @* The menu already names the page; the heading is kept for screen readers and search. *@
    <p class="ta-page-idea">What do you own?</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">A few hundred shares of one company. A few hundred of another. A big chunk in index funds. Cash spread out between three banks and a brokerage firm. But why, exactly, do you own this combination?</p>
    <p class="ta-page-statement">Do you have a plan?</p>

    <h2 class="ta-page-statement">No? Then build one: your own personal index.</h2>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">Everyone who owns an index fund of the 500 largest US companies buys the same risk. But the risk appetite of someone in their 30s isn’t the same as someone in their 60s. Select how many companies you want to own and how much you want to keep in cash. It’s that simple. Fewer companies means more risk; more cash means less risk. Once you’ve chosen, your index is weighted by company size and rebalanced every quarter, like the big index funds.</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">Do you want all the benefits of an index fund without owning every company in it? Maybe your employer has restrictions, maybe you don’t like the brand. Maybe you believe in ESG, or maybe your faith drives your decisions. Leave out the companies you can’t own or don’t want.</p>

    <p class="ta-page-sub">Here’s what that looks like for Ken.</p>

    <div class="ta-holdings">
        <div class="ta-holdings-name">Ken today</div>
        <div class="ta-holdings-bar" role="img" aria-label="Ken today: JPM $60,000, DIS $40,000, SPY $200,000, QQQ $120,000, cash $80,000">
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c1" style="width: 12%"><b>JPM</b>$60k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c2" style="width: 8%"><b>DIS</b>$40k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c3" style="width: 40%"><b>SPY</b>$200k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c4" style="width: 24%"><b>QQQ</b>$120k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-cash" style="width: 16%"><b>Cash</b>$80k</div>
        </div>
        <p class="ta-caption">Over the last five years</p>
        <div class="ta-plan-stats">
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Annual return</span><span class="ta-plan-value">10.7%</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Volatility</span><span class="ta-plan-value">15.6%</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Sharpe ratio</span><span class="ta-plan-value">0.48</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Worst drop</span><span class="ta-plan-value">−26%</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>$500,000 grew to</span><span class="ta-plan-value">$830k</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="ta-page-sub">Ken has a few companies he doesn’t want to own. His paycheck already depends on Google, and he doesn’t want his retirement to depend on it too. He believes in Tesla, but doesn’t like the price swings, and he hasn’t used social media in years. So GOOGL, GOOG, TSLA and META stay out of his index.</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">Now suppose Ken had built his own personal index from the largest US companies. He would remove his four tickers and keep the 11 largest that are left. Here’s how his portfolio would have performed.</p>

    <div class="ta-holdings">
        <div class="ta-holdings-name">The Ken 11</div>
        <div class="ta-holdings-bar" role="img" aria-label="The Ken 11: NVDA $111,000, AAPL $101,000, MSFT $75,000, 8 more companies $207,000, cash $6,000">
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c1" style="width: 22.2%"><b>NVDA</b>$111k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c3" style="width: 20.2%"><b>AAPL</b>$101k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-c2" style="width: 15%"><b>MSFT</b>$75k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-rest" style="width: 41.5%"><b>8 more</b>$207k</div>
            <div class="ta-holdings-seg ta-holdings-cash" style="width: 1.1%"></div>
        </div>
        <p class="ta-caption">Over the last five years</p>
        <div class="ta-plan-stats ta-plan-stats-better">
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Annual return</span><span class="ta-plan-value">15.5%</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Volatility</span><span class="ta-plan-value">15.6%</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Sharpe ratio</span><span class="ta-plan-value">0.76</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>Worst drop</span><span class="ta-plan-value">−18%</span></div>
            <div class="ta-plan-stat"><span>$500,000 grew to</span><span class="ta-plan-value">$1,029k</span></div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="ta-page-statement">Higher returns. Same risk. Smaller drops.</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">By not having a plan, Ken takes the same risk, earns less for it, and drops further in a bad year. The Sharpe ratio tells the story in one number: it measures how much you’re paid for the risk you take; the higher, the better. Ken’s ratio rises from 0.48 to 0.76 with a plan.</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">Why not just own an index fund and be done with it? Over the same five years, the index fund would have earned Ken about $67,000 more than his original portfolio, but only by taking more risk than he has today. His own personal index would have earned him about $199,000 more, at the same risk.</p>
    <p class="ta-page-sub">We don’t promise the next five years will look like the last five. A plan isn’t a forecast, but having one beats not having one.</p>

    <div class="ta-page-final">
        <p class="ta-page-close">Your index. Your shares. Your plan.</p>
        <AuthorizeView>
            <NotAuthorized>
                <a href="/signup" class="ta-btn ta-btn-primary ta-btn-large">Sign up</a>
            </NotAuthorized>
        </AuthorizeView>
    </div>
</div>

<section class="ta-band ta-fineprint">
    <div class="ta-container">
        <p class="ta-figures"><strong>About these figures.</strong> These are backtested results from October 22, 2021 to October 23, 2026: they represent a hypothetical illustration, not a prediction or a recommendation. Ken today is his current mix, bought once and held. The Ken 11 holds the 11 largest companies other than the four he leaves out, reweighted by market value each quarter. Every two weeks its cash is reset so its volatility stays at Ken’s; it averaged 22% and is 1% today. The index fund is a fund of the 500 largest US companies. Cash earns nothing in either portfolio. Sharpe ratios use the return on short-term Treasury bills as the risk-free rate. Returns include dividends but not taxes or trading costs.</p>
    </div>
</section>
